WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.820
In this video, we will follow an agent’s
program through Submilli:

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:08.260
why the language and packages are designed
for agents,

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:12.580
how the program runs, and how the
application controls its access.

00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:16.140
An agent needs to total a customer’s
charges.

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:19.700
It writes a short program: fetch the
charges,

00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:23.780
add them up, return the total. Submilli
gives that program a

00:00:23.860 --> 00:00:26.900
place to run. The language, compiler,

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:30.980
and runtime are designed together around
code an agent writes and

00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:35.300
may need to repair. Let’s follow that
program and see what those choices

00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:39.460
buy us.  Start with the language.
TypeScript gives the

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:42.660
agent familiar functions, loops, objects,

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:46.820
and type annotations. Submilli implements
its own version so

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:49.980
it can change the rules where that helps
generated code.

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:52.900
There is no any type to bypass checking.

00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:57.460
Raw JSON arrives as unknown. When the
program casts it

00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:01.700
to a specific type, Submilli checks the
value’s shape at runtime.

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:05.300
If an amount arrives as text instead of a
number,

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:09.860
the cast fails there. The agent gets a
failure at the bad assumption,

00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:12.500
before that value travels through the
calculation.

00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:15.940
Familiar syntax helps it write the
program;

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:18.340
stricter checks help it repair one.

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:23.680
The program uses Submilli’s standard
library and installed packages.

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:28.240
Why not accept any N P M dependency? Those
libraries may

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:31.280
expect Node’s filesystem, process model,

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:34.880
or other facilities that this runtime does
not provide.

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:39.120
Supporting them would mean carrying those
expectations into the execution

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:42.000
environment. With Submilli packages,

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:45.600
you choose the available integrations
before the run.

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:49.680
As a package author, you expose typed
functions and place permission

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:52.400
checks before the actions they protect.

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:56.480
Here, list charges calls check with the
customer ID.

00:01:56.560 --> 00:01:58.720
If the blueprint refuses permission,

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:01.040
check throws a permission denied error,

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:04.080
and the function stops before reading the
charges.

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:07.120
You do not need to start a package from
scratch:

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:11.120
ask your coding assistant to use the
Submilli skill to create one,

00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:14.280
then review the operations and checks it
defines.

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:17.880
Dependencies are pinned and recorded in a
lockfile.

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:21.920
The agent can compose the operations you
provided without assembling

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:24.480
a new software environment for each task.

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:29.120
That trades unrestricted library choice
for an interface the application

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:33.360
can prepare and control.  The compiler
also helps the agent fix

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:36.960
its work. Here, the program misspells
amount.

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:41.360
The diagnostic marks the field, suggests
the correct spelling,

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:45.520
and includes the type declaration. The
agent has a concrete correction

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:48.880
to make. Once the program passes these
checks,

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:51.280
Submilli compiles it to WebAssembly.

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:55.280
Package modules are built ahead of the run
and linked alongside it.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:58.160
Both execute through the same runtime.

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:03.040
This gives Submilli a defined instruction
format to execute and meter,

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:06.160
while the agent continues to write
readable TypeScript.

00:03:07.433 --> 00:03:11.433
Your application sends the program to the
Submilli server over H T

00:03:11.513 --> 00:03:15.673
T P or M C P. Inside that existing
process,

00:03:15.753 --> 00:03:19.913
the runtime creates an execution instance
and runs the compiled code

00:03:19.993 --> 00:03:22.153
with Submilli’s WebAssembly interpreter.

00:03:22.713 --> 00:03:26.553
Each instance holds the program’s state
and has resource limits.

00:03:26.793 --> 00:03:31.433
The program reaches external services
through supported host operations.

00:03:31.513 --> 00:03:35.273
These are functions the runtime provides
for work outside the WebAssembly

00:03:35.353 --> 00:03:38.873
program, such as sending an H T T P
request.

00:03:38.953 --> 00:03:42.793
A package calls that runtime function to
contact the service;

00:03:42.873 --> 00:03:46.153
the program does not open a network
connection on its own.

00:03:46.233 --> 00:03:49.913
This arrangement puts execution and its
controls together,

00:03:50.113 --> 00:03:53.513
without starting a separate operating
system for each program.

00:03:54.767 --> 00:03:57.367
That matters when you run many agents.

00:03:57.647 --> 00:04:01.407
A micro V M provides a hardware
virtualization boundary,

00:04:01.487 --> 00:04:04.367
with a guest operating system and memory
to provision.

00:04:05.007 --> 00:04:09.247
Submilli creates WebAssembly instances
inside the process it already

00:04:09.327 --> 00:04:13.247
has. Compilation and instance setup still
take work,

00:04:13.327 --> 00:04:16.767
but there is no guest operating system to
boot for each run.

00:04:17.007 --> 00:04:19.327
When one program waits for a service,

00:04:19.367 --> 00:04:22.607
the runtime can yield while other runs
make progress.

00:04:22.847 --> 00:04:26.207
It is a different isolation boundary from
a micro V M,

00:04:26.287 --> 00:04:30.487
so the runtime and its host operations
remain part of what you trust.

00:04:30.847 --> 00:04:33.607
Useful failures are another part of the
design.

00:04:33.887 --> 00:04:37.487
Submilli’s GitHub package can report that
a branch has moved since

00:04:37.527 --> 00:04:41.487
the agent read it. The agent can read the
current branch and reconsider

00:04:41.607 --> 00:04:45.647
its edit. A rate limit error can carry
retry timing when the service

00:04:45.727 --> 00:04:49.247
provides it. The caller can decide when to
try again;

00:04:49.327 --> 00:04:52.127
the package does not retry automatically.

00:04:52.287 --> 00:04:56.287
These errors distinguish a stale
assumption from a temporary limit.

00:04:56.407 --> 00:05:00.127
That gives the agent a specific next step
instead of repeating the

00:05:00.287 --> 00:05:04.447
same failed call. Purpose-built packages
can make the operation,

00:05:04.527 --> 00:05:08.367
its permission checks, and its recovery
information one documented

00:05:08.447 --> 00:05:09.407
interface.

00:05:10.700 --> 00:05:14.940
Now return to our billing example. Listing
charges first checks

00:05:15.020 --> 00:05:17.900
the requested operation and customer ID.

00:05:18.220 --> 00:05:22.140
The blueprint you created compares that
requested ID with the customer

00:05:22.220 --> 00:05:25.020
ID in the context your application
supplied.

00:05:25.580 --> 00:05:29.740
The rule contains a comparison, not a
particular customer’s ID.

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:33.900
For this run, the application binds
Customer A’s ID.

00:05:34.460 --> 00:05:37.580
A request for Customer A matches and can
proceed.

00:05:38.140 --> 00:05:41.020
A request for Customer B does not match,

00:05:41.100 --> 00:05:43.420
so the check refuses it before the lookup.

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:48.700
On a run for Customer B, the application
binds that ID instead;

00:05:48.780 --> 00:05:51.420
the same blueprint now permits Customer B.

00:05:51.900 --> 00:05:55.540
The check sits inside the package, so it
is part of performing the

00:05:55.580 --> 00:06:00.380
operation. The generated program cannot
choose the trusted context

00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:03.180
or decide whether its own request is
allowed.

00:06:04.433 --> 00:06:08.673
These choices work together. Familiar
TypeScript gives the agent

00:06:08.753 --> 00:06:11.073
a practical way to express the task.

00:06:11.553 --> 00:06:16.113
Stricter checks and specific errors help
it find and repair mistakes.

00:06:16.353 --> 00:06:20.433
WebAssembly lets the runtime execute and
meter the program inside

00:06:20.473 --> 00:06:24.273
an existing process. Packages provide the
operations,

00:06:24.353 --> 00:06:26.673
and blueprints define their permitted use.

00:06:27.073 --> 00:06:30.513
The result is an environment built for
generated code:

00:06:30.593 --> 00:06:34.433
the agent can compose useful work and
respond to failures,

00:06:34.513 --> 00:06:38.753
while your application defines the access
and resources it receives.
